1 ABSTRACT

     The FDL editor (EDIT/FDL) writes FDL files: the file definitions
     that CREATE/FDL and CONVERT/FDL make files from. You can edit a
     definition line by line (ADD, DELETE, MODIFY) or have a script ask
     you about the file and its use and design it for you (INVOKE):
     sequential, relative and indexed files, keys added, removed or
     reworked, and an indexed file tuned with what ANALYZE/RMS_FILE/FDL
     found in the data (/ANALYSIS).

     An FDL file has sections, each a primary attribute (FILE, RECORD,
     AREA n, KEY n...) followed by its secondary attributes and their
     values. EXIT writes the definition as a new version of the file
     named on the command line (or /OUTPUT).
1 ADD

     Adds a line to the definition: you choose the primary attribute
     (the section) and the secondary attribute, then give its value. A
     section that isn't there yet is made. If the line is there already,
     you can replace it.

     Format

       ADD
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: ADD
     Enter Desired Primary: FILE
     Enter FILE Attribute: ALLOCATION
     Enter value for this Secondary: 300
1 DELETE

     Removes a line from the definition, and its section with it when it
     was the last line there.

     Format

       DELETE
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: DELETE
     Enter Desired Primary: FILE
     Enter FILE Attribute: ALLOCATION
1 EXIT

     Writes the definition to the FDL file and leaves the editor. Ctrl/Z
     at the main menu does the same. Nothing is written when the
     definition is empty.

     Format

       EXIT
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: EXIT
1 HELP

     Shows these topics. Return at the main menu asks for them too; Return
     at the Topic? prompt goes back to the main menu.

     Format

       HELP
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: HELP
     Topic? INVOKE
1 INVOKE

     Runs a script: questions about the file and how it will be used,
     after which the editor works out the definition. A script you leave
     with Ctrl/Z changes nothing you had.

       SEQUENTIAL   a sequential file
       RELATIVE     a relative file
       INDEXED      an indexed file, its keys and their buckets
       ADD_KEY      another key for an indexed file
       DELETE_KEY   the indexed file's last key removed
       TOUCHUP      one key of an indexed file reworked
       OPTIMIZE     an indexed file reworked with what ANALYZE/RMS_FILE
                    found in its data (EDIT/FDL/ANALYSIS=)

     For an indexed file's keys the editor shows a plot of how deep the
     index gets with each bucket size; you can change what it was told
     (by the two-letter names under the plot) and watch the plot follow,
     then finish the design (FD).

     Format

       INVOKE
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: INVOKE
     Editing Script Title: INDEXED
1 MODIFY

     Changes the value of a line already in the definition.

     Format

       MODIFY
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: MODIFY
     Enter Desired Primary: RECORD
     Enter RECORD Attribute: SIZE
     Enter value for this Secondary: 80
1 OPERATION

     The editor asks one question at a time. Each shows what it takes
     (keywords, a range of numbers, Yes/No) and the answer Return gives,
     in brackets; [-] means there is no such answer and you must give
     one. Keywords can be shortened while they stay unambiguous.
2 FILES

     The definition is read from the file named on the command line (a
     new one is started if it doesn't exist) and written there as a new
     version, or to /OUTPUT. /ANALYSIS names the output of
     ANALYZE/RMS_FILE/FDL for the OPTIMIZE script.
2 PROMPT-LEVEL

     The questions come with short explanations when an answer isn't
     accepted.
2 QUESTION-FORMAT

     Question                        (what it takes)[default]        :
2 CONTROL_CHARACTERS

     Ctrl/Z at a question goes back to the main menu, dropping what a
     script had asked so far; at the main menu it is EXIT. "?" at a
     keyword question lists the keywords.
1 QUIT

     Leaves the editor without writing anything.

     Format

       QUIT
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: QUIT
1 SET

     Sets the editor's characteristics, those the command line's
     qualifiers set too: the analysis file, the emphasis, the
     granularity, the output file, the number of keys, the plot.

     Format

       SET
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: SET
     Editor characteristic: OUTPUT
1 VIEW

     Shows the definition as EXIT would write it.

     Format

       VIEW
2 EXAMPLE

     Main Editor Function: VIEW
